{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"}
      ],
      "why": "This is the only family that has produced feasible designs (29/41) and it owns 11/11 of the current merged front, with best feasible area 39973 um^2. It gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps >= 32 needs only Fmax >= 32 MHz, which one registered micro-rotation per stage meets with margin; the binding constraints are accuracy and the 0.5 us / 32-request batch budget, and the objective is area, so I search right at the accuracy boundary. data_width 12..20 brackets the necessary W (output LSB 2^-(W-2), so W = 13 is the hard floor for 2^-12 with rounding and 12 is deliberately included as the infeasible side of the boundary); n_iter 10..20 brackets the residual-rotation floor N ~ 13 while still letting the search confirm it; angle_guard -2..3 and frac_guard 0..3 span cheap-but-lossy truncation to safe rounding so the optimiser can buy the last bits of accuracy in whichever currency is cheapest in cells. This family gets the larger share because the trade-off curve of the whole spec currently lives here."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 2, "param": "m"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"}
      ],
      "why": "Same arithmetic, 1 result/cycle, but only ceil(N/m) register banks instead of N, so it is the natural place to shave flip-flops and area below the pipelined front. My earlier 59 evals found 0 feasible, which points at the combination of m and the batch/latency budgets rather than accuracy (accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, which are searched identically here). I therefore restrict m to 2..4: with m chained micro-rotations between registers the clock period grows roughly linearly in m while the cycle count only falls as N/m, so latency_ns = (ceil(N/m)+2+31) x period and throughput_msps = Fmax both degrade for large m; large m is the most likely cause of the earlier all-infeasible batch and is a liability at L2 as well. The accuracy ranges are kept identical to pipelined so that any feasible point found here is directly comparable point-for-point on area and can form lower-area, lower-Fmax Pareto points that pipelined alone cannot reach. Smaller share than pipelined because it is unproven on this spec."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-dominated: throughput >= 32 msps is trivially satisfied by any 1-result/cycle pipeline, and the error bound (2^-12) is loose enough that small W/N designs sit near the boundary. So the useful work is mapping the accuracy-versus-area frontier right at the feasibility edge, which is exactly where the current merged front (all pipelined, best area 39973) already lives. Round 1 therefore spends most of the 100-eval slice on pipelined for a dense map of the edge, with data_width and n_iter deliberately reaching one step into infeasible territory (W=12, N=10) to pin down where the constraint actually bites, and angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding opened wide because those are the cheap knobs that trade cells for the last fraction of a bit. pipelined_m gets a substantial minority slice with m capped at 2..4: it is the only route to fewer flip-flops and hence lower area at the same throughput, but the earlier all-infeasible result suggests its failure mode is the system batch/latency budget at large m, so I keep m small rather than re-spending budget on the region that already failed. If pipelined_m yields feasible points in round 1 I will widen m upward next round; if it again yields none I will fold its share back into pipelined and push the accuracy boundary tighter."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "This is the final round (round cap reached), and the front is feasible and well-covered, so neither `infeasible` nor further exploration applies. All three constraints are satisfiable simultaneously: 0% of evaluations violate throughput_msps >= 32 (best 190), and the Pareto front contains designs meeting max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (best front point 2^-12.58 at area 42524 um^2) and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.5 (best seen 0.221). The feasible front holds 15 designs spanning area_um2 42524..74284 (HV ref 100000) and accuracy_bits 12.6..17.4 (HV ref 12), with hypervolume 2.311e+05 and a +21.0% gain this round — the curve is broad and still improving, but the budget/round cap is reached. The selection rule (min area_um2) is cleanly served by the pipelined [data_width=19 n_iter=14 angle_guard=3 frac_guard=0 rounding=trunc] point at area_um2=42524, accuracy_bits=12.6, throughput 123 MSPS — comfortably inside every constraint with margin on the system batch bound. pipelined_m contributed 0 feasible designs (max throughput 91.1 MSPS, best accuracy 13.06 bits), so it does not extend the front. Stopping here leaves the best mapped front for the spec's selection rule."
  ,"next_plan": null
}
